From: meenmachine1 <78238681+meenmachine1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 01:13:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Decompiler: an option to fold a signed flag test of a
 decrement's result into one comparison

- Shows: a byte-count loop ending `subs r2,r2,#1; bgt loop` (the
  source's `while (--n > 0)`, or `n--` at the end of `while (n > 0)`)
  decompiles as `iVar2 = n + -1; bVar1 = 0 < n; n = iVar2; } while
  (iVar2 != 0 && bVar1);`: two tests, one of them on the value before
  the decrement, kept in a temporary.
- Cause: GT after SUBS is `!Z && N == V`. The rules turn `N == V` into a
  signed comparison of the value before the subtraction (`0 < n`) and Z
  into a test of the result (`n - 1 != 0`), and nothing combines a
  comparison of one varnode with a test of another (RuleLessNotEqual and
  RuleRangeMeld need the same varnode).
- Patch: a new rule, RuleFlagResultCompare, behind a new option,
  `flagcompare` (off by default): `(V + d != 0) && (c < V)` with c + d ==
  -1 becomes `0 < V + d`, and the negation `(V + d == 0) || (V < c)` with
  c + d == 0 becomes `V + d <= 0` (the less-or-equal forms of the second
  comparison too). It assumes the decrement doesn't overflow (V isn't
  within d of the minimum), as the source code does; that is why it is
  an option.
- Seen on: ARMv5TE (Nintendo DS, CodeWarrior) code in a matching
  decompilation; the same flag pattern on Thumb and other flag-setting
  processors.
- Ghidra 12.1.4.

